%erg 0.1
Title: A ticket body that has fallen behind its own log
Created: 2026-09-11
Author: claude

--- log ---
2026-09-11T16:07Z claude created

--- body ---
## Context

Filed from the roar sweep after PR #524, which corrected ticket 0727. The
defect there was not in the work: it was that 0727's **body** still asserted a
mechanism its **log** had refuted five days earlier, and still carried an
Action that a control had shown to be impossible to run. A reader who trusted
the body would re-run refuted work.

That is exactly what happened, to this session, in front of the author. I read
0727's body, recommended running its Action 1 as the cheap way to unblock the
sitter release, and was wrong twice over: `update_url` is REQUIRED (the build
Action 1 asks for is refused at install, control run 2026-09-06), and the
mechanism Action 1 isolates was refuted the same evening.

An `.erg` file has an append-only log and a mutable body. The log is therefore
always the newer of the two, and nothing makes the body follow it. The longer
and more valuable a ticket's investigation, the further apart they drift.

## The sweep

Heuristic over the 128 open tickets: log matches
`REFUTED|NOT SUPPORTED BY|is wrong as stated|superseded|does not hold`, while
the body matches none of `refuted|superseded|void|read the log|corrected|no
longer`. Four candidates: **0025, 0491, 0606, 0722**.

Positive control: 0727 matches the log half and, since #524, also the body half,
so it correctly drops out of the list. The sweep is not blind.

These four are **candidates, not confirmed defects**. The words can appear in a
log for reasons unrelated to that ticket's own claims, so each needs reading
before anything is changed. 0606 is worth reading first: it is `needs-human`,
it now blocks 0767, and a stale body there misdirects a decision waiting on the
author.

## Actions

1. Read each of 0025, 0491, 0606, 0722 and decide, per ticket, whether the body
   contradicts its log. Correct only those that do.
2. Where one does, use 0727's shape rather than inventing another: a short
   section ahead of the filed text naming the supersessions, and refuted
   actions marked void and left unstruck. Deleting a refuted arm is how it gets
   proposed again.
3. Decide whether a standing check is worth its cost. A candidate shape: an
   adherence test carrying the sweep above, failing when an open ticket's log
   records a refutation and its body carries no marker. Weigh it against
   `rules/workflow.md`'s guard budget — a guard whose false-positive rate is
   high is a tax on every future ticket, and the heuristic above is textual, so
   it will mis-hit.

## Test

If step 3 produces a guard, its red step is 0727 **as it stood before #524** —
a real body that really had fallen behind, available at `origin/main~1`. A
guard that has never failed against a known-bad fixture is not a check.

## Invariants

- The log stays append-only. Corrections go in the body, or as a new log entry,
  never by editing a past one.
- Do not mass-edit the four candidates on the strength of the grep. The sweep
  ranks reading order; it does not decide.

## Exit criteria

Each of the four candidates has been read and either corrected or recorded as a
false hit, and the standing-guard question of Action 3 is answered either way —
built, or declined with its reason.
